      <!-- About -->

      <span class="section-label">Overview</span>
      <h2 class="content-title">About Annapurna Circuit Trek</h2>


  <div class="title-divider"></div>

  <div class="about-text">
        <p>The 12 Days Annapurna Circuit Trek is one of the most popular long-distance treks in Nepal, offering a unique experience across varied landscapes, high mountain passes, and countless Himalayan villages in the heart of the country.</p>
        <p>The well-planned journey starts with a scenic drive from Kathmandu to Chame, the district headquarters of Manang, following the Marsyangdi River valley. From there, the trail slowly climbs through pine forests, past fantastic rock formations, and under the massive Paungda Danda cliff before reaching Upper Pisang. In the upper Manang valley, where Tibetan influence becomes stronger, trekkers have their first encounter with the arid, high-altitude landscape.</p>
        <p>The trail then passes through Ngawal, a traditional village of stone houses and ancient monasteries. It is far less visited than other villages on the Lower Circuit, has a relaxed atmosphere, and offers views of Annapurna II and Pisang Peak.</p>
        <p>Next comes Manang, an important acclimatization stop and one of the most culturally lively villages on the circuit. Here trekkers can adapt to the altitude while exploring glacial lakes, ice formations, and Tibetan Buddhist monasteries in and around the valley, making the crossing of the high pass ahead safer and more enjoyable.</p>
        <p>Beyond Manang, the landscape opens into a wide, high alpine terrain at Yak Kharka and Thorong High Camp, surrounded by snow-capped peaks. The crossing of Thorong La Pass (5,416m), one of the highest trekking passes in the world, is what this trek is all about, both as a challenge and as a reward.</p>
        <p>The early morning climb is strenuous, but the views of the Annapurna and Damodar Himal ranges are unmatched. The descent leads into the sacred pilgrimage site of Muktinath, revered by both Hindus and Buddhists.</p>
        <p>From here, the route follows the dramatic Kali Gandaki Valley, often described as the deepest gorge in the world, through Kagbeni and Jomsom to the whitewashed stone houses and narrow alleyways of Marpha, a village of apple growers. Along the way, trekkers can enjoy side trips to the tranquil Dhumba Lake and the Green Lake and Blue Lake, hidden gems of the region. The trail continues to Kobang and Kalopani, a serene village with close-up views of Dhaulagiri and Nilgiri, before the trek concludes with a drive back to Pokhara.</p>
        <p>In the past, the Annapurna Circuit was one of the great classic treks of Nepal, traditionally walked as a full circuit around the Annapurna massif over two to three weeks. Growing road access to Chame and Kalopani in recent years has made this 12-day version far more time-efficient, while the cultural flavor and mountain views remain intact. It is a perfect choice for trekkers with limited time who still want a true high-altitude experience.</p>
        <p>The trail is at its best in spring (March–May) and autumn (September–November), when skies are clear and the views of Annapurna, Dhaulagiri, and other Himalayan giants are unobstructed. Beyond Manang, the rain-shadow landscape also makes some sections of the route possible outside of the main trekking seasons.</p>
        <p>Overall, the Annapurna Circuit Trek is ideal for those looking for a full-fledged Himalayan adventure, with high altitudes, rich Tibetan-influenced culture, and some of the most dramatic landscapes in Nepal, from river valleys to the highland desert.</p>
      </div>

